    "faq": {
      "type": "faq-accordion",
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What are wudhu socks?",
            "answer": "<p>Waterproof socks built to the physical properties the masah conditions rest on: water does not pass through them, they hold their shape rather than collapsing against the foot, and they stay in place through normal wear. HydroSox states those properties. It does not issue rulings on them.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are HydroSox certified for wudu?",
            "answer": "<p>No. No certificate has been issued, by us or by anyone else. A brand cannot award itself one. What we publish is what the sock is made of and how it behaves, so that you — or the scholar you follow — can judge it.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is the difference between these and ordinary waterproof socks?",
            "answer": "<p>Nothing in the membrane, and everything in the structure. It is the same Porelle® laminate in every HydroSox pair. What matters for masah is that the sock holds its shape as a covering over the foot, and that is built into the construction rather than added for this page.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are these the same as leather socks or khuffs?",
            "answer": "<p>Not the same material, and the same intent. Khuffain are the leather footwear the classical rulings were written about. These are a modern waterproof construction aimed at the same physical properties. Whether that equivalence holds is a question for scholarship, and the masah page sets out how it is discussed.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can women wear these?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. The sock is unisex and the bands start at UK 3. Size on foot length rather than on the label you are used to.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How many pairs do I need for daily use?",
            "answer": "<p>Most people performing wudu daily keep three. They are washed cool and air-dried, which takes longer than a tumble dryer would, so a rotation of three keeps a dry pair available every day.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I wash them without damaging the waterproofing?",
            "answer": "<p>Cool wash, inside out, no fabric softener, no bleach, then air dry away from any heat source. Heat and softener end a membrane far faster than wearing it does.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Where can I read about the masah conditions themselves?",
            "answer": "<p>On the masah page, which sets out the conditions, how masah is performed, how long it lasts, and where the schools differ — with every source named. We report that scholarship. We do not add to it.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
